TI  - Using Language Processing and Speech Analysis for the Identification of Psychosis 
      and Other Disorders.
PG  - 770-779
LID - S2451-9022(20)30154-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bpsc.2020.06.004 [doi]
AB  - Increasingly, data-driven methods have been implemented to understand 
      psychopathology. Language is the main source of information in psychiatry and 
      represents "big data" at the level of the individual. Language and behavior are 
      amenable to computational natural language processing (NLP) analytics, which may 
      help operationalize the mental status examination. In this review, we highlight 
      the application of NLP to schizophrenia and its risk states as an exemplar of its 
      use, operationalizing tangential and concrete speech as reductions in semantic 
      coherence and syntactic complexity, respectively. Other clinical applications are 
      reviewed, including forecasting suicide risk and detecting intoxication. 
      Challenges and future directions are discussed, including biomarker development, 
      harmonization, and application of NLP more broadly to behavior, including 
      intonation/prosody, facial expression and gesture, and the integration of these 
      in dyads and during discourse. Similar NLP analytics can also be applied beyond 
      humans to behavioral motifs across species, important for modeling 
      psychopathology in animal models. Finally, clinical neuroscience can inform the 
      development of artificial intelligence.
